        "heading": "Executing One End to End: A Compromised Account",
        "content": "NIST Special Publication 800-61r2 describes the incident response lifecycle in four phases: Preparation; Detection and Analysis; Containment, Eradication and Recovery; and Post-Incident Activity. (In April 2025 NIST published Revision 3, which recasts incident response around the six functions of the Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover — instead of a fixed four-phase lifecycle. The four phases below are still the clearest way to learn the mechanics and map cleanly onto Rev 3's Detect, Respond and Recover functions, so they remain how this lesson teaches it.) Playbooks are how those phases become something you can actually do at a keyboard. Here is the compromised-account playbook mapped onto them.\n\n**Preparation** happened before your shift started. It is the logging being switched on, the alert rule existing, the playbook being written, and you having the permissions to do what the playbook asks. If you reach a step and discover you lack the access to perform it, that is a preparation failure, and it belongs in the post-incident review rather than being quietly worked around.\n\n**Detection and Analysis** is the collect-and-decide half. The alert says an impossible-travel sign-in for a user. You pull seven days of Entra ID sign-in logs and look at `IPAddress`, `Location`, `DeviceDetail` and `ResultType`. You check whether the successful sign-in was preceded by failures, and from where. You check whether MFA methods or the password changed recently, because an attacker who registers their own MFA method has converted a stolen password into durable access. You check the Exchange audit log for inbox rules created around the same time — a rule that forwards mail to an external address and deletes the original from the inbox is one of the strongest single indicators in this entire incident type. You check OAuth consents. Only now do you have a position.\n\n**Containment, Eradication and Recovery** is where the playbook's authority section earns its place, because every containment action costs something and someone has to accept that cost.\n\nDisabling the account stops the attacker — and simultaneously destroys your visibility into what they would have done next, tells them plainly that they have been detected, and locks out a real human being who may be mid-deadline. Revoking sessions and forcing a password reset is usually the better first move: it invalidates the attacker's stolen tokens without necessarily announcing itself as an incident response.\n\nIsolating a host preserves its memory for forensics and stops it talking to a command-and-control server — and also stops the user working, which in a hospital or a factory is not a small thing.\n\nThere is no free action. This is the single most important idea in the section: containment is always a trade, and the playbook's function is to record in advance who is entitled to make that trade at what scale.\n\nEradication removes what the attacker left: the forwarding rule, the rogue OAuth consent, the attacker-registered MFA method. Recovery restores normal service and verifies it stayed normal.\n\n**Post-Incident Activity** is the phase most SOCs skip and the only one that makes next month easier. It is covered later in this lesson.",
        "codeExample": "flowchart TD\n  A[Alert: suspicious sign-in for a user] --> B[Collect sign-in logs, MFA changes, inbox rules, OAuth consents]\n  B --> C{Does the user confirm the sign-in was theirs?}\n  C -->|Yes, and the evidence agrees| D[Close as benign true positive and document why]\n  C -->|No, or user unreachable| E{New inbox rule, new MFA method, or new OAuth consent?}\n  E -->|No| F[Monitor, raise sign-in risk, recheck in 24h]\n  E -->|Yes| G[Confirmed compromise]\n  G --> H{More than 3 accounts, OR a privileged role, OR mail exfiltration?}\n  H -->|Yes| I[STOP and escalate to IR manager: scope exceeds this playbook]\n  H -->|No| J[Contain: revoke sessions and force password reset]\n  J --> K[Eradicate: remove rule, revoke consent, remove rogue MFA method]\n  K --> L[Recover, notify user, write timeline, close]"
